## 8. Closing and decision control

The ability to lead a conversation should ultimately help the prospect make an informed decision.

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Trial Close | Testing purchase readiness before asking for a final decision. |
| Assumptive Close | Discussing next steps when the prospect has already indicated readiness. |
| Summary Close | Recapping the value and agreed-upon benefits before asking for a decision. |
| Alternative Close | Offering two reasonable ways to proceed. |
| Commitment Question | Asking a question that clarifies willingness to move forward. |
| Decision Frame | Helping the prospect understand the decision in front of them. |
| Value Alignment | Connecting the solution to the prospect's priorities. |
| Risk Reversal | Reducing legitimate concerns about purchasing. |
| Buying Temperature | An informal assessment of how ready a prospect is to purchase. |
| Decision Momentum | The progress made toward a purchasing decision. |
| Next-Step Agreement | Establishing a mutually agreed-upon action. |
| Ethical Urgency | Communicating genuine reasons why timing matters. |

